{# The currency the site is priced in, and a panel to change it.

   This used to be a `soon` placeholder in `site.main-menu` -- a span reading
   "CAD" that said "coming soon" to a screen reader. It is not in that menu any
   more, and it should not go back: the map is keyed by URL and both its readers
   use the key as an href, so an entry that must never become a link would be a
   special case in the header and in the footer forever. This is a control, not
   a destination.

   Hand-rolled on the passenger selector's shape (search/passengers.html.twig):
   a button owning `aria-expanded`, a panel it names with `aria-controls`,
   hidden with the `hidden` attribute so the keyboard skips it while it is shut.
   There is no Bootstrap dropdown anywhere in this app and this is not the place
   to introduce the first one.

   The two data attributes are how global.js learns the cookie to write and how
   long to write it for, so src/Currency.php stays the only place either is
   spelled -- the same contract the cookie notice uses. #}
<div class="currency">
    <button type="button" class="currency__trigger js-currency-trigger"
            aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="currency-panel">
        <i class="fas fa-globe fa-lg me-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>{{ currency.active }}
        <span class="visually-hidden"> &mdash; change currency</span>
    </button>

    <div class="currency__panel" id="currency-panel"
         data-cookie="{{ currency.cookie }}"
         data-max-age="{{ currency.max_age }}"
         hidden>
        {# Thirty is too many to scan and too few to page, which is what a
           filter is for. `type="search"` rather than text: it comes with a
           clear button on the platforms that draw one, and announces itself. #}
        <label class="visually-hidden" for="currency-filter">Search currencies</label>
        <input class="currency__filter js-currency-filter" id="currency-filter"
               type="search" placeholder="Search" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">

        <ul class="currency__list js-currency-list">
            {% for code, item in currency.list %}
                {% set is_active = code == currency.active %}
                <li class="currency__item">
                    {# `data-search` is built here rather than read off the text
                       in the browser, so matching does not depend on which
                       elements happen to hold which words.

                       Folded to ASCII first, and the map is two characters long
                       because only two names in the whole catalogue need it:
                       Icelandic Króna and Polish Złoty. Without it "kron" finds
                       the Swedish, Norwegian and Danish krona and silently
                       misses the Icelandic one -- which looks like a bug in the
                       list rather than in the search. #}
                    <button type="button"
                            class="currency__option js-currency-choice{{ is_active ? ' is-active' : '' }}"
                            data-code="{{ code }}"
                            data-search="{{ (code ~ ' ' ~ item.name)|lower|replace({'ó': 'o', 'ł': 'l'}) }}"
                            {% if is_active %}aria-current="true"{% endif %}>
                        <span class="currency__code">{{ code }}</span>
                        <span class="currency__name">{{ item.name }}</span>
                        {# The symbol repeats what the name says, so it is
                           decorative -- announcing "kr" after "Swedish Krona"
                           is noise. #}
                        <span class="currency__symbol" aria-hidden="true">{{ item.symbol }}</span>
                        {% if is_active %}
                            <i class="fas fa-check currency__tick" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                            <span class="visually-hidden"> (selected)</span>
                        {% endif %}
                    </button>
                </li>
            {% endfor %}
        </ul>

        {# What the numbers on the page are, and how old they are. The line the
           whole feature is honest because of: a converted price is an estimate
           at somebody else's rate, and this is where a reader can see whose and
           when. `rates_date()` is lazy, so a visitor who never opens this pays
           nothing for it.

           No date means no rates have ever been fetched and the built-in ones
           are in use. It says that rather than naming a date, because a
           confident wrong date is worse than an admission. #}
        {% set published = rates_date() %}
        <p class="currency__source">
            {% if published %}
                European Central Bank rates, {{ published|date('j F Y') }}
            {% else %}
                Built-in reference rates
            {% endif %}
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
